1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,408
Modulation is the answer to radio's founding problem: how do you make a wave carry

2
00:00:07,408 --> 00:00:07,936
a voice?

3
00:00:07,936 --> 00:00:09,518
Because here's the bind.

4
00:00:09,518 --> 00:00:14,795
The wave you can actually radiate — a radio-frequency carrier — is a steady tone

5
00:00:14,795 --> 00:00:15,982
that says nothing.

6
00:00:15,982 --> 00:00:21,324
And the thing you want to send — audio, data — is a baseband signal that can't be

7
00:00:21,324 --> 00:00:22,379
radiated at all.

8
00:00:22,379 --> 00:00:25,677
Two signals, and neither one can do the job alone.

9
00:00:26,127 --> 00:00:31,239
The way across the gap is this: there are only three things about a sinusoidal

10
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:32,747
carrier you can change.

11
00:00:32,747 --> 00:00:34,779
Its amplitude — how tall it is.

12
00:00:34,779 --> 00:00:37,073
Its frequency — how fast it cycles.

13
00:00:37,073 --> 00:00:39,630
Its phase — the timing of those cycles.

14
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:44,677
Modulation is varying one or more of them in step with the message, and every

15
00:00:44,677 --> 00:00:48,086
scheme ever invented is a way of doing exactly that.

16
00:00:48,086 --> 00:00:51,494
The reverse process at the receiver is demodulation.

17
00:00:51,944 --> 00:00:53,206
Watch the classics.

18
00:00:53,206 --> 00:00:58,454
AM — amplitude modulation — writes the message into the carrier's envelope: the

19
00:00:58,454 --> 00:01:00,513
wave's height traces the voice.

20
00:01:00,513 --> 00:01:05,561
FM shifts the carrier's frequency with the message instead — and because its

21
00:01:05,561 --> 00:01:11,074
envelope stays constant, amplitude noise mostly bounces off, which is why FM sounds

22
00:01:11,074 --> 00:01:12,136
cleaner than AM.

23
00:01:12,136 --> 00:01:17,450
Phase modulation nudges the carrier's timing; it's closely related to FM, and it

24
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:19,509
underlies most digital schemes.

25
00:01:19,959 --> 00:01:23,381
Analog modulation varies a property continuously.

26
00:01:23,381 --> 00:01:29,176
Digital modulation makes one sharp break with that: it switches the carrier among a

27
00:01:29,176 --> 00:01:33,227
finite set of symbols, each standing for one or more bits.

28
00:01:33,227 --> 00:01:36,089
FSK toggles between discrete frequencies.

29
00:01:36,089 --> 00:01:38,603
PSK toggles between discrete phases.

30
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:42,723
QAM picks from combinations of amplitude and phase at once.

31
00:01:43,173 --> 00:01:48,277
Those symbols are naturally drawn on a constellation diagram — the map of the IQ

32
00:01:48,277 --> 00:01:51,785
plane the receiver uses to decide which symbol arrived.

33
00:01:51,785 --> 00:01:55,613
And the constellation makes radio's central bargain visible.

34
00:01:55,613 --> 00:02:00,653
Pack more bits per symbol — more constellation points — and spectral efficiency

35
00:02:00,653 --> 00:02:01,035
rises.

36
00:02:01,035 --> 00:02:06,203
But the points crowd closer together, so noise blurs them into each other sooner:

37
00:02:06,203 --> 00:02:09,520
it takes more signal-to-noise to keep them distinct.

38
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:14,623
That's the fundamental power-versus-rate trade, and Shannon capacity puts a hard

39
00:02:14,623 --> 00:02:15,516
ceiling on it.

40
00:02:15,516 --> 00:02:20,492
Choosing a modulation scheme is a trade among data rate, bandwidth, power, and

41
00:02:20,492 --> 00:02:24,065
robustness — there is no free lunch anywhere on the map.

42
00:02:24,515 --> 00:02:29,093
So real systems pick their point on the trade deliberately.

43
00:02:29,093 --> 00:02:35,533
Land-mobile digital voice has to survive weak, fading links, so it runs low, robust

44
00:02:35,533 --> 00:02:41,973
orders: C4FM four-level FSK and π/4-DQPSK, precisely because they hold up at modest

45
00:02:41,973 --> 00:02:48,103
SNR — with pulse shaping keeping each symbol from smearing into its neighbours.

46
00:02:48,103 --> 00:02:54,544
High-throughput systems — LTE, Wi-Fi, DVB — climb to 16-, 64-, or 256-QAM over OFDM

47
00:02:54,544 --> 00:02:59,354
when the channel is good, and adapt downward when it degrades.

48
00:02:59,804 --> 00:03:05,213
Recognising a signal's modulation and applying the matching demodulator is the

49
00:03:05,213 --> 00:03:07,779
heart of everything GopherTrunk does.

50
00:03:07,779 --> 00:03:13,049
Its chain identifies the trunking waveform, then runs the appropriate symbol

51
00:03:13,049 --> 00:03:18,596
recovery — four-level FSK slicing for DMR and NXDN, π/4-DQPSK carrier-and-symbol

52
00:03:18,596 --> 00:03:19,775
tracking for P25.

53
00:03:19,775 --> 00:03:25,392
And notice the loop closing: the three carrier properties reappear as the axes of

54
00:03:25,392 --> 00:03:31,008
the IQ plane, so once the samples are in software, demodulation is just measuring

55
00:03:31,008 --> 00:03:36,278
amplitude, frequency, and phase — and mapping them back to symbols and bits.

56
00:03:36,728 --> 00:03:41,837
So: modulation is the bridge between message and carrier — three properties to

57
00:03:41,837 --> 00:03:47,076
vary, analog varies them smoothly, digital snaps them to an alphabet of symbols,

58
00:03:47,076 --> 00:03:51,137
and every extra bit per symbol is bought with signal-to-noise.

59
00:03:51,137 --> 00:03:52,905
Full write-up linked below.

